    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "let left = 0;\nlet best = 0;\n\nfor (let right = 0; right < arr.length; right++) {\n  // 1. the new element enters\n  add(arr[right]);\n\n  // 2. shrink while the window is invalid\n  while (isInvalid()) {\n    remove(arr[left]);\n    left++;\n  }\n\n  // 3. the window is valid here — record it\n  best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Almost every variable window solution is this exact structure. What changes is the definition of `add`, `remove`, and `isInvalid`."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Why the nested loop is still O(N)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "A `while` inside a `for` looks quadratic. It is not, and the reason is the same as with two pointers."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "`right` advances exactly N times total. `left` also only ever advances, and it can never pass `right`, so it advances at most N times across the **entire run** — not per iteration. Total movement is bounded by 2N, giving **O(N)**."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "This is called amortised analysis: individual steps vary, but the total is bounded. Watch for it whenever a nested loop has a pointer that never resets."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Longest versus shortest"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The two variants differ in **where you record the answer**:"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Longest valid window** — shrink until valid, then record. The window is valid at the bottom of the loop.",
        "**Shortest valid window** — grow until valid, then record *inside* the shrink loop while it still holds, trying to make it smaller."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Getting this backwards is the classic bug. Ask yourself: at the moment I record, is the window guaranteed valid? If not, the answer will be wrong."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Window state"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Variable windows usually need more than a running sum — typically a frequency map of what is currently inside. Both `add` and `remove` must keep it accurate, and forgetting to delete a key when its count hits zero is a frequent source of subtle bugs."
    }
